export default "# HTML Attributes and Their Usage\r\n\r\n*A four-part lesson. Running example: building out the Harbor Goods product page, a small online shop that sells outdoor and boating gear, and learning how attributes control links, images, forms, styling, accessibility, and behaviour.*\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 1 of 4: What Attributes Are\r\n\r\n### 1.1 The idea\r\n\r\nAn **attribute** gives an element extra information or settings. The element says *what* something is, and its attributes say *how it should behave* or *which details apply*.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<a href=\"products.html\" class=\"button\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">See products</a>\r\n```\r\n\r\nHere the element is `<a>` (a link), and it has four attributes: `href`, `class`, `target`, and `rel`.\r\n\r\n### 1.2 The syntax\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<img src=\"images/life-jacket.jpg\" alt=\"Red life jacket\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\">\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Rule | Detail |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Location | Attributes go only in the **opening tag** (never in the closing tag) |\r\n| Format | `name=\"value\"` |\r\n| Separation | Separate attributes with spaces; the order does not matter |\r\n| Quotes | Always quote values. Double quotes are the convention |\r\n| Names | Lowercase by convention (HTML is not case-sensitive for names) |\r\n| Values | Usually case-sensitive for things like `id`, `class`, and file paths; keywords such as `type=\"email\"` are not |\r\n| Repeats | An attribute may appear only once on an element; if repeated, the browser ignores the later copies |\r\n\r\nQuotes matter when the value has spaces:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<p class=\"intro large\">Text</p>   <!-- correct: two classes -->\r\n<p class=intro large>Text</p>     <!-- wrong: \"large\" becomes a separate attribute -->\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf the value itself contains a double quote, use single quotes around it, or write `&quot;`:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<p title='She said \"welcome\"'>Hover over me</p>\r\n<p title=\"She said &quot;welcome&quot;\">Hover over me</p>\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 1.3 Kinds of attributes\r\n\r\n| Kind | Example | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Value attribute** | `href=\"about.html\"` | Takes text, a number, a URL, or a keyword |\r\n| **Boolean attribute** | `required`, `disabled`, `checked`, `hidden` | Present means on, absent means off |\r\n| **Enumerated attribute** | `type=\"email\"`, `method=\"post\"`, `contenteditable=\"true\"` | The value must be one of a fixed set of keywords |\r\n| **Global attribute** | `id`, `class`, `lang`, `title`, `hidden`, `tabindex`, `data-*` | Allowed on any element |\r\n| **Element-specific attribute** | `src` on `<img>`, `for` on `<label>`, `action` on `<form>` | Only meaningful on certain elements |\r\n| **ARIA attribute** | `role`, `aria-label`, `aria-hidden` | Accessibility information for assistive technology |\r\n| **Event handler attribute** | `onclick`, `onsubmit` | Run JavaScript; generally avoid (see Part 3) |\r\n| **Custom data attribute** | `data-product-id=\"42\"` | Your own data, attached to an element |\r\n\r\n### 1.4 Boolean attributes in detail\r\n\r\nA boolean attribute is **on if it is present**, whatever its value.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\" checked>\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\" checked=\"checked\">\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\" checked=\"\">\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\" checked=\"false\">   <!-- STILL checked! -->\r\n```\r\n\r\nAll four boxes are checked. To turn it off, **remove the attribute**.\r\n\r\n| Common boolean attribute | Effect |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `checked` | A checkbox or radio is ticked |\r\n| `selected` | An `<option>` is chosen by default |\r\n| `disabled` | The control cannot be used or focused, and its value is not submitted |\r\n| `readonly` | The value cannot be edited, but it can be focused and is submitted |\r\n| `required` | A value must be provided before the form submits |\r\n| `multiple` | Allows several files or options |\r\n| `autofocus` | Focus this control on page load (use sparingly) |\r\n| `hidden` | The element is not shown |\r\n| `controls` | Shows built-in media controls |\r\n| `autoplay`, `loop`, `muted` | Media behaviour |\r\n| `defer`, `async` | Script loading |\r\n| `open` | A `<details>` or `<dialog>` starts open |\r\n| `novalidate` | A form skips built-in validation |\r\n\r\n### 1.5 Enumerated attributes and a common trap\r\n\r\nSome attributes accept a fixed set of keywords, and an invalid value falls back to a default.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Allowed values (examples) | Default if wrong or missing |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `type` on `<input>` | `text`, `email`, `password`, `number`, `date`, `checkbox`, `radio`, `file`, `search`, `tel`, `url`, `hidden`, and more | `text` |\r\n| `method` on `<form>` | `get`, `post`, `dialog` | `get` |\r\n| `type` on `<button>` | `submit`, `button`, `reset` | `submit` |\r\n| `loading` on `<img>` | `lazy`, `eager` | `eager` |\r\n| `dir` | `ltr`, `rtl`, `auto` | Inherited |\r\n| `contenteditable`, `draggable`, `spellcheck` | `true`, `false` | Varies |\r\n\r\nNote that `contenteditable`, `draggable`, and `spellcheck` are **not** boolean attributes. They need `true` or `false`, which is the opposite of how `checked` works.\r\n\r\nA very common trap: a `<button>` inside a form with no `type` is a **submit** button. Always write `type=\"button\"` for buttons that should not submit the form.\r\n\r\n### 1.6 Where to find the list of valid attributes\r\n\r\nThere are far too many to memorise. Use the reference when unsure.\r\n\r\n| Resource | Use |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| MDN Web Docs (developer.mozilla.org) | Best reference for every element and attribute, with examples and browser support |\r\n| HTML Living Standard (html.spec.whatwg.org) | The official specification |\r\n| W3C Validator | Tells you when an attribute is not allowed on an element |\r\n| Browser developer tools | Shows the attributes the browser actually applied |\r\n\r\n**Part 1 summary.** You can write attributes correctly, tell value, boolean, enumerated, global, ARIA, event, and custom attributes apart, avoid the `checked=\"false\"` trap, and know where to look up the rest.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 2 of 4: The Attributes You Will Use Most\r\n\r\n### 2.1 Global attributes\r\n\r\nThese work on almost any element.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `id` | A **unique** identifier for one element | `<h2 id=\"sizes\">` |\r\n| `class` | One or more reusable labels, used for styling and scripting | `<div class=\"card featured\">` |\r\n| `style` | Inline CSS (avoid; prefer a stylesheet) | `<p style=\"color: red\">` |\r\n| `title` | Extra information, often shown as a tooltip | `<abbr title=\"Personal flotation device\">PFD</abbr>` |\r\n| `lang` | The language of this element's content | `<span lang=\"fr\">bon voyage</span>` |\r\n| `dir` | Text direction | `<p dir=\"rtl\">` |\r\n| `hidden` | Hides the element | `<div hidden>` |\r\n| `tabindex` | Controls keyboard focus | `<div tabindex=\"0\">` |\r\n| `accesskey` | A keyboard shortcut (avoid; conflicts with assistive tools) | |\r\n| `contenteditable` | Lets the user edit the content | `<p contenteditable=\"true\">` |\r\n| `draggable` | Allows drag and drop | |\r\n| `translate` | Whether the content should be translated | `<span translate=\"no\">Harbor Goods</span>` |\r\n| `inert` | Makes an element and its children non-interactive | `<div inert>` |\r\n| `data-*` | Custom data | `<li data-product-id=\"42\">` |\r\n| `aria-*`, `role` | Accessibility semantics | `<nav aria-label=\"Main\">` |\r\n\r\n### 2.2 `id` and `class` in depth\r\n\r\n| | `id` | `class` |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Uniqueness | Must be **unique** on the page | Can repeat on many elements |\r\n| How many per element | One | Several, separated by spaces |\r\n| Valid characters | No spaces; letters, digits, hyphens, underscores are safest; case-sensitive | Same |\r\n| Uses | Link targets (`#sizes`), `<label for>`, ARIA relationships, scripts | Styling groups of elements, scripts |\r\n| CSS selector | `#sizes` | `.card` |\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<h3 id=\"sizes\">Sizes</h3>\r\n<a href=\"#sizes\">Jump to sizes</a>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"card featured\">...</div>\r\n<div class=\"card\">...</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nGood naming: describe the **purpose**, not the look (`class=\"warning\"` rather than `class=\"red-box\"`), use lowercase with hyphens, and avoid starting an `id` with a digit. Duplicated `id` values break links, labels, and scripts, so check for them in the validator.\r\n\r\n### 2.3 Link attributes (`<a>`)\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<a href=\"https://example.com/guide.pdf\"\r\n   target=\"_blank\"\r\n   rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\r\n   download\r\n   hreflang=\"en\"\r\n   type=\"application/pdf\">Download the guide (PDF)</a>\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `href` | Where the link goes | An `<a>` without `href` is a placeholder, not a link |\r\n| `target` | Where to open it | `_self` (default), `_blank` (new tab), `_parent`, `_top` |\r\n| `rel` | The relationship to the target | `noopener`, `noreferrer`, `nofollow`, `sponsored`, `ugc`, `external` |\r\n| `download` | Download instead of navigating | Optionally gives a file name: `download=\"guide.pdf\"`; works for same-origin URLs |\r\n| `hreflang` | Language of the target | A hint |\r\n| `type` | MIME type of the target | A hint |\r\n| `referrerpolicy` | What referrer information to send | e.g. `no-referrer` |\r\n\r\nLink types you can write in `href`:\r\n\r\n| Link | Example |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Another page on your site | `href=\"about.html\"` or `href=\"/about.html\"` |\r\n| Another site | `href=\"https://example.com\"` |\r\n| Section of the same page | `href=\"#sizes\"` (needs a matching `id`) |\r\n| Email | `href=\"mailto:hello@harborgoods.example\"` |\r\n| Phone | `href=\"tel:+15551234567\"` |\r\n\r\nOn `target=\"_blank\"` links, modern browsers treat `noopener` as the default, but being explicit with `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` is a good habit. Add `nofollow` or `sponsored` when linking to paid or untrusted content.\r\n\r\n### 2.4 Image attributes (`<img>`)\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<img src=\"images/life-jacket-800.jpg\"\r\n     srcset=\"images/life-jacket-400.jpg 400w,\r\n             images/life-jacket-800.jpg 800w,\r\n             images/life-jacket-1600.jpg 1600w\"\r\n     sizes=\"(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw\"\r\n     alt=\"Red adult life jacket with a front zip and reflective strips\"\r\n     width=\"800\" height=\"600\"\r\n     loading=\"lazy\"\r\n     decoding=\"async\">\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `src` | The default image file (required) |\r\n| `alt` | A text alternative (required for accessibility; use `alt=\"\"` for decorative images) |\r\n| `width`, `height` | The image's intrinsic size in pixels, so the browser reserves space and avoids layout shift |\r\n| `srcset` | A list of image files and their widths, so the browser can choose the best one |\r\n| `sizes` | How wide the image will be displayed at different screen sizes |\r\n| `loading` | `lazy` delays off-screen images; `eager` loads at once (leave the first visible image eager) |\r\n| `decoding` | `async` lets the browser decode off the main thread |\r\n| `fetchpriority` | `high` or `low` hint for important or unimportant images |\r\n| `crossorigin`, `referrerpolicy` | Control cross-origin loading and referrer details |\r\n\r\nWrite alt text for the image's **purpose**: \"Red adult life jacket with a front zip\", not \"image of jacket\" or a file name. If the image is decorative, use an empty alt. If the image is a link or button, the alt should describe the destination or action.\r\n\r\n### 2.5 Form attributes\r\n\r\n**On the `<form>`:**\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `action` | The URL that receives the data |\r\n| `method` | `get` (data in the URL; for searches) or `post` (data in the body; for changes and sensitive data) |\r\n| `enctype` | How data is encoded; use `multipart/form-data` for file uploads |\r\n| `novalidate` | Skip the browser's built-in validation |\r\n| `autocomplete` | Default autofill behaviour |\r\n| `target` | Where to show the response |\r\n\r\n**On the controls (`<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, `<button>`):**\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose | Notes |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `name` | The key used when the value is submitted | A control with no `name` is **not submitted** |\r\n| `id` | Unique identifier, linked to the label's `for` | |\r\n| `type` | Kind of input | Chooses keyboard, validation, and appearance |\r\n| `value` | The initial or submitted value | For checkboxes and radios it is what is sent when selected |\r\n| `placeholder` | A short hint shown when empty | Not a replacement for a label |\r\n| `required` | Must be filled in | |\r\n| `disabled` | Cannot be used; **not submitted** | |\r\n| `readonly` | Cannot be edited; **is submitted** | |\r\n| `autofocus` | Focus on load | Use rarely |\r\n| `autocomplete` | Autofill hint | Use standard tokens such as `name`, `email`, `current-password`, `new-password`, `one-time-code`, `street-address` |\r\n| `inputmode` | Which on-screen keyboard to show | `numeric`, `decimal`, `tel`, `email`, `url`, `search` |\r\n| `enterkeyhint` | The label of the Enter key on mobile | `search`, `send`, `go`, `next`, `done` |\r\n| `form` | Associates a control with a form elsewhere in the page | Uses the form's `id` |\r\n\r\n**Validation attributes:**\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Applies to | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `required` | Most controls | A value is mandatory |\r\n| `minlength`, `maxlength` | Text inputs, textarea | Limits on the number of characters |\r\n| `min`, `max` | `number`, `range`, `date`, `time` | Limits on the value |\r\n| `step` | `number`, `range`, `date`, `time` | Allowed increments (`step=\"any\"` allows decimals) |\r\n| `pattern` | Text-like inputs | A regular expression the value must match |\r\n| `multiple` | `email`, `file` | Allows several values |\r\n| `accept` | `file` | Suggested file types, such as `image/*` or `.pdf` |\r\n| `title` | With `pattern` | Often used to explain the pattern, but do not rely on it alone |\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<form action=\"/subscribe\" method=\"post\">\r\n  <p>\r\n    <label for=\"email\">Email address</label><br>\r\n    <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\"\r\n           required autocomplete=\"email\" inputmode=\"email\"\r\n           placeholder=\"you@example.com\">\r\n  </p>\r\n\r\n  <p>\r\n    <label for=\"qty\">Quantity (1 to 10)</label><br>\r\n    <input type=\"number\" id=\"qty\" name=\"qty\" min=\"1\" max=\"10\" step=\"1\" value=\"1\">\r\n  </p>\r\n\r\n  <p>\r\n    <label for=\"code\">Order code (3 letters, 4 digits)</label><br>\r\n    <input type=\"text\" id=\"code\" name=\"code\"\r\n           pattern=\"[A-Za-z]{3}[0-9]{4}\" maxlength=\"7\"\r\n           title=\"Three letters followed by four digits, such as ABC1234\">\r\n  </p>\r\n\r\n  <p>\r\n    <label for=\"photo\">Upload a photo</label><br>\r\n    <input type=\"file\" id=\"photo\" name=\"photo\" accept=\"image/*\">\r\n  </p>\r\n\r\n  <button type=\"submit\">Subscribe</button>\r\n</form>\r\n```\r\n\r\nRemember: **client-side validation is only a convenience.** Anyone can bypass it, so the server must validate again.\r\n\r\n### 2.6 Labels and linking attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Used on | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `for` | `<label>` | Matches the `id` of the control it describes |\r\n| `id` | The control | Must equal the label's `for` |\r\n| `name` | Radio buttons | Radios with the **same `name`** form one group (only one can be selected) |\r\n| `aria-describedby` | Any | Points to the `id` of help or error text |\r\n| `aria-labelledby` | Any | Points to the `id` of an element that names this one |\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\r\n<input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" aria-describedby=\"email-help\">\r\n<small id=\"email-help\">We only use this to send your receipt.</small>\r\n```\r\n\r\nWrapping also works (`<label>Email <input ...></label>`), but using `for` and `id` is the most robust.\r\n\r\n### 2.7 Media and embedding attributes\r\n\r\n| Element | Attribute | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `<video>`, `<audio>` | `src`, `controls`, `autoplay`, `loop`, `muted`, `preload`, `poster` (video) | Playback and appearance; autoplay with sound is usually blocked and is poor for accessibility |\r\n| `<video>` | `playsinline` | Plays inside the page on iOS |\r\n| `<source>` | `src`, `type`, `media`, `srcset` | Alternative files for the browser to choose from |\r\n| `<track>` | `kind`, `src`, `srclang`, `label`, `default` | Captions, subtitles, and descriptions |\r\n| `<iframe>` | `src`, `title`, `width`, `height`, `loading`, `sandbox`, `allow`, `referrerpolicy` | Embedding another page; always add a `title` |\r\n\r\n### 2.8 Resource-linking attributes (head and scripts)\r\n\r\n| Element | Attribute | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `<link>` | `rel`, `href`, `type`, `media`, `as`, `crossorigin`, `integrity` | Stylesheets, icons, preloads |\r\n| `<meta>` | `charset`, `name`, `content`, `property`, `http-equiv` | Page information |\r\n| `<script>` | `src`, `defer`, `async`, `type`, `integrity`, `crossorigin`, `nonce`, `referrerpolicy` | JavaScript loading and safety |\r\n| `<base>` | `href`, `target` | Base URL for relative links (use with care) |\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\">\r\n<script src=\"js/main.js\" defer></script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 2.9 Table attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Used on | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `scope` | `<th>` | `col` or `row`: which cells the header describes |\r\n| `colspan`, `rowspan` | `<td>`, `<th>` | Cells that stretch across columns or rows |\r\n| `headers` | `<td>` | Links a cell to header cell `id`s (complex tables) |\r\n\r\n### 2.10 Custom data attributes (`data-*`)\r\n\r\n`data-*` attributes let you attach your own information to elements without breaking validity.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<li class=\"product\" data-product-id=\"42\" data-price=\"45.00\" data-in-stock=\"true\">\r\n  Coastal Pro\r\n</li>\r\n```\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst item = document.querySelector(\".product\");\r\nconsole.log(item.dataset.productId);   // \"42\"  (data-product-id becomes productId)\r\nconsole.log(item.dataset.price);       // \"45.00\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n\r\n- Names must start with `data-`, use lowercase, and hyphens become camelCase in JavaScript.\r\n- Values are always **strings**.\r\n- Do not store anything sensitive: visitors can read it in the source.\r\n- Use data attributes for small hooks (IDs, flags, settings), not for content that users need to read.\r\n\r\n### 2.11 ARIA attributes (accessibility)\r\n\r\n**ARIA** (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes add accessibility information when plain HTML cannot express it.\r\n\r\n**First rule of ARIA: if a native HTML element or attribute does the job, use that instead.** A real `<button>` is better than `<div role=\"button\">`, because the native element already gives keyboard support, focus, and the right announcement.\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Purpose | Example |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `role` | Declares what an element is | `role=\"alert\"`, `role=\"dialog\"` |\r\n| `aria-label` | A text name when no visible label exists | `<button aria-label=\"Close\">×</button>` |\r\n| `aria-labelledby` | Name taken from another element | `aria-labelledby=\"title-id\"` |\r\n| `aria-describedby` | Extra description from another element | `aria-describedby=\"help-id\"` |\r\n| `aria-hidden=\"true\"` | Hides from assistive technology (never on focusable elements) | A decorative icon |\r\n| `aria-expanded` | Whether something is open | A menu button, `true` or `false` |\r\n| `aria-controls` | Which element this one controls | |\r\n| `aria-current` | Marks the current item | `aria-current=\"page\"` in a nav |\r\n| `aria-live` | Announces changes automatically | `aria-live=\"polite\"` for status messages |\r\n| `aria-invalid`, `aria-required` | Form states (prefer native `required`) | |\r\n\r\nWrong ARIA is worse than none. Test with a keyboard and, ideally, a screen reader.\r\n\r\n### 2.12 `tabindex` and keyboard focus\r\n\r\n| Value | Effect |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `tabindex=\"0\"` | Puts a non-interactive element into the natural tab order |\r\n| `tabindex=\"-1\"` | Focusable by script (or a link target), but skipped by the Tab key |\r\n| `tabindex=\"1\"` or higher | Forces a custom order, which **confuses keyboard users**; avoid |\r\n\r\nNative interactive elements (`<a href>`, `<button>`, form controls) are already focusable, so they need no `tabindex`.\r\n\r\n### 2.13 Standard `rel` values\r\n\r\n| Value | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `stylesheet` | An attached stylesheet |\r\n| `icon` | The site icon |\r\n| `canonical` | The preferred URL for the page |\r\n| `preload`, `preconnect`, `dns-prefetch` | Performance hints |\r\n| `noopener` | The new page cannot access `window.opener` |\r\n| `noreferrer` | Do not send the `Referer` header (also implies `noopener`) |\r\n| `nofollow` | Do not pass ranking credit to this link |\r\n| `sponsored` | The link is paid or an ad |\r\n| `ugc` | The link is in user-generated content |\r\n\r\n**Part 2 summary.** You can use the global attributes, `id` and `class` properly, add the right attributes to links, images, forms, media, scripts, tables, custom data, ARIA, and focus management, and choose the correct `rel` values.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 3 of 4: Using Attributes Well, Safely, and Accessibly\r\n\r\n### 3.1 Attributes versus CSS versus JavaScript\r\n\r\n| Need | Best tool |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Say what something is and give its data (URL, alt text, input type) | **HTML attribute** |\r\n| Control how it looks (colour, spacing, layout) | **CSS** |\r\n| React to the user or change things dynamically | **JavaScript** |\r\n| Hook CSS or JS onto elements | `class`, `id`, and `data-*` |\r\n\r\nOld HTML had presentation attributes (`bgcolor`, `align`, `border`, `<font color>`). They are obsolete. Use CSS instead.\r\n\r\n| Obsolete or discouraged | Use instead |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `bgcolor=\"red\"` | CSS `background-color` |\r\n| `align=\"center\"` | CSS `text-align` or flexbox |\r\n| `border=\"1\"` on tables | CSS `border` |\r\n| `<font color>` | CSS `color` |\r\n| Inline `style=\"...\"` everywhere | A stylesheet with classes |\r\n| `onclick=\"...\"` event attributes | `addEventListener` in a script file |\r\n| `accesskey` | Avoid; it conflicts with assistive technology shortcuts |\r\n\r\n### 3.2 The difference between an attribute and a property\r\n\r\nThis trips up many learners when they start with JavaScript.\r\n\r\n| | Attribute | Property |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Lives in | The HTML text | The live DOM object |\r\n| Example | `<input value=\"Medium\">` | `input.value` |\r\n| Changes when the user types? | The attribute keeps the **initial** value | The property reflects the **current** value |\r\n| Read and write | `getAttribute(\"value\")`, `setAttribute(\"value\", \"L\")` | `input.value` |\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nconst input = document.querySelector(\"#qty\");\r\ninput.getAttribute(\"value\");  // the original value from the HTML\r\ninput.value;                  // what the user has typed now\r\ninput.setAttribute(\"disabled\", \"\");   // turn a boolean attribute on\r\ninput.removeAttribute(\"disabled\");    // turn it off\r\ninput.toggleAttribute(\"hidden\");      // flip a boolean attribute\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor boolean attributes, use the properties (`input.checked`, `input.disabled`) or `toggleAttribute`, not `setAttribute(\"checked\", \"false\")`.\r\n\r\n### 3.3 Reading and changing attributes with JavaScript\r\n\r\n| Method | Purpose |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `element.getAttribute(\"name\")` | Read an attribute |\r\n| `element.setAttribute(\"name\", \"value\")` | Set an attribute |\r\n| `element.removeAttribute(\"name\")` | Remove an attribute |\r\n| `element.hasAttribute(\"name\")` | Check if it exists |\r\n| `element.toggleAttribute(\"name\")` | Add if absent, remove if present |\r\n| `element.classList.add(\"x\")`, `.remove`, `.toggle`, `.contains` | Work with the `class` attribute safely |\r\n| `element.dataset.name` | Read and write `data-*` attributes |\r\n| `element.id`, `element.title`, `element.hidden` | Common attributes as properties |\r\n\r\nExample: toggle a menu and tell assistive technology about it.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<button type=\"button\" id=\"menu-btn\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"menu\">Menu</button>\r\n<ul id=\"menu\" hidden>\r\n  <li><a href=\"/products.html\">Products</a></li>\r\n  <li><a href=\"/about.html\">About</a></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<script>\r\n  const btn = document.querySelector(\"#menu-btn\");\r\n  const menu = document.querySelector(\"#menu\");\r\n  btn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n    const open = btn.getAttribute(\"aria-expanded\") === \"true\";\r\n    btn.setAttribute(\"aria-expanded\", String(!open));\r\n    menu.hidden = open;\r\n  });\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.4 Attribute selectors in CSS\r\n\r\nCSS can select elements by their attributes.\r\n\r\n| Selector | Matches |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `[disabled]` | Any element with a `disabled` attribute |\r\n| `input[type=\"email\"]` | Email inputs |\r\n| `a[href^=\"https\"]` | Links whose `href` **starts with** `https` |\r\n| `a[href$=\".pdf\"]` | Links whose `href` **ends with** `.pdf` |\r\n| `a[href*=\"example\"]` | Links whose `href` **contains** `example` |\r\n| `[data-in-stock=\"false\"]` | Elements with that data value |\r\n| `[lang|=\"en\"]` | `lang` equal to `en` or starting with `en-` |\r\n\r\n```css\r\na[target=\"_blank\"]::after { content: \" ↗\"; }\r\ninput:required { border-color: #0b3d5c; }\r\ninput:invalid  { border-color: #b00020; }\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.5 Accessibility checklist for attributes\r\n\r\n| Practice | Attributes involved |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Set the page language | `lang` on `<html>` |\r\n| Give every informative image a description | `alt` |\r\n| Mark decorative images | `alt=\"\"` |\r\n| Connect every form control to a label | `for` and `id` |\r\n| Help with errors and instructions | `aria-describedby`, `aria-invalid` |\r\n| Use the right input types | `type`, `inputmode`, `autocomplete` |\r\n| Name unlabelled icon buttons | `aria-label` |\r\n| Mark the current page in navigation | `aria-current=\"page\"` |\r\n| Hide decorative content from assistive technology | `aria-hidden=\"true\"` (never on focusable elements) |\r\n| Announce dynamic status messages | `aria-live` or `role=\"status\"` |\r\n| Give iframes and tables names | `title`, `<caption>` |\r\n| Keep focus order logical | Avoid positive `tabindex` |\r\n| Provide captions | `<track kind=\"captions\">` |\r\n| Do not block zoom | Avoid `user-scalable=no` in the viewport tag |\r\n\r\n### 3.6 Security view of attributes\r\n\r\nAttributes are a common route for attacks, because they carry URLs, scripts, and user data.\r\n\r\n| Risk | How it happens | Safer habit |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **Cross-site scripting through attributes** | Untrusted text placed into an attribute without escaping can break out of the quotes and add event handlers (for example a value of `\" onmouseover=\"alert(1)`) | Escape output for the **attribute context** on the server; in JavaScript use `setAttribute` or properties and `textContent`, never string-building with `innerHTML` |\r\n| **Dangerous URL schemes** | `href` or `src` set to `javascript:...` runs code when clicked | Never use `javascript:` URLs; validate that user-supplied URLs start with `http:`, `https:`, or `mailto:` |\r\n| **Inline event handlers** | `onclick=\"...\"` mixes code into HTML and blocks a strict Content Security Policy | Use `addEventListener` in external scripts |\r\n| **Reverse tabnabbing** | A `target=\"_blank\"` link lets the new page access the opener | Use `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` |\r\n| **Leaking referrer information** | Full URLs sent to other sites | `referrerpolicy` on links and resources, or a site-wide policy |\r\n| **Tampered third-party files** | A CDN file changes | `integrity` (Subresource Integrity) with `crossorigin=\"anonymous\"` and a pinned version |\r\n| **Untrusted embeds** | An iframe can do too much | `sandbox` (and only the permissions it needs), `title`, and `allow` limited to what is required |\r\n| **Trusting hidden or client-side values** | `<input type=\"hidden\">`, `readonly`, `disabled`, `data-*`, `min`, `max`, and `pattern` can all be edited by the user | Re-validate everything on the server; never trust a price or role from the browser |\r\n| **Secrets in attributes** | API keys or tokens in `data-*`, `value`, or hidden inputs are public | Keep secrets on the server only |\r\n| **Weak password fields** | A visible password, or autofill blocked | Use `type=\"password\"` with proper `autocomplete` values (`current-password`, `new-password`) so password managers work; `autocomplete=\"off\"` is often ignored for passwords anyway |\r\n| **Uploads** | `accept` does not stop malicious files | Validate file type, size, and content on the server |\r\n| **Cross-origin images and scripts** | Credentials or data leaks to other origins | Set `crossorigin` and `referrerpolicy` deliberately |\r\n| **Autoplay and permissions** | Annoying or abusive media and device access | Avoid `autoplay`; restrict `allow` on iframes |\r\n\r\nA dangerous and a safer way to put user text into an attribute with JavaScript:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// Unsafe: the string could contain a quote and inject new attributes or handlers\r\ncontainer.innerHTML = '<a href=\"' + userUrl + '\" title=\"' + userTitle + '\">link</a>';\r\n\r\n// Safer: build the element and set attributes as data, after validating the URL\r\nconst a = document.createElement(\"a\");\r\nconst url = new URL(userUrl, location.href);\r\nif (url.protocol === \"https:\" || url.protocol === \"http:\") {\r\n  a.href = url.href;\r\n}\r\na.title = userTitle;          // set as a property; no HTML parsing\r\na.textContent = \"link\";\r\ncontainer.append(a);\r\n```\r\n\r\n### 3.7 Performance and attributes\r\n\r\n| Attribute | Benefit |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `width` and `height` on images and videos | Reserves space, preventing layout shift |\r\n| `loading=\"lazy\"` on below-the-fold images and iframes | Saves data and speeds up the first view |\r\n| `srcset` and `sizes` | The browser downloads the right-sized image |\r\n| `decoding=\"async\"` | Keeps the page responsive while images decode |\r\n| `fetchpriority=\"high\"` on the main hero image | Loads the most important image sooner |\r\n| `defer` on scripts | Does not block rendering |\r\n| `preload`, `preconnect` in `<link rel>` | Starts important downloads early |\r\n| `type` and `media` on `<source>` and `<link>` | Lets the browser skip files it cannot use |\r\n\r\nDo not put `loading=\"lazy\"` on the image at the top of the page that users see first, since that delays the most important content.\r\n\r\n### 3.8 Validation and debugging\r\n\r\n| Problem | How to investigate |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| An attribute seems ignored | Check spelling; check it is allowed on that element (MDN); check quotes; look in **Elements** to see whether the browser kept it |\r\n| `checked=\"false\"` still checks the box | Remove the attribute |\r\n| Label does not focus its field | `for` must exactly match the control's `id` (case-sensitive) |\r\n| Radio buttons all selectable at once | Give them the **same `name`** |\r\n| A field's value is not sent | The control has no `name`, or it is `disabled` |\r\n| Link target does not jump | The `id` is missing, misspelled, or duplicated |\r\n| Image does not load | Check `src` path, case, and the Network tab for a 404 |\r\n| Page jumps while images load | Add `width` and `height` |\r\n| `pattern` seems not to work | Patterns must match the **whole** value; test the expression; it only applies to text-like inputs |\r\n| Form submits when a button is clicked | The button defaults to `type=\"submit\"`; set `type=\"button\"` |\r\n| Unknown attribute warning | Use `data-*` for custom data, or check for a typo |\r\n\r\nIn the browser's **Elements** panel you can double-click an attribute to edit it live, or right-click an element and choose **Edit attribute** to experiment. Changes vanish when you reload.\r\n\r\n### 3.9 Common mistakes\r\n\r\n| Mistake | Problem | Fix |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Putting attributes in the closing tag | Ignored or invalid | Only the opening tag |\r\n| Forgetting quotes | Values with spaces split into several attributes | Always quote |\r\n| Duplicate `id` values | Broken links, labels, and scripts | Make each `id` unique; use `class` for repeats |\r\n| Missing `alt` on images | Inaccessible | Always provide it (empty if decorative) |\r\n| `checked=\"false\"`, `disabled=\"false\"` | Still on | Remove the attribute |\r\n| Wrong or missing `for` and `id` pair | The label is not connected | Match them exactly |\r\n| Controls without a `name` | Values are not submitted | Add `name` |\r\n| Using `placeholder` as the only label | Disappears when typing; poor accessibility | Add a real `<label>` |\r\n| Using `title` as the only accessible name or for essential information | Unreliable on touch and for keyboard users | Visible text and proper labels |\r\n| Positive `tabindex` values | Confusing keyboard order | Use `0` or `-1`, or fix the DOM order |\r\n| Wrong ARIA, or ARIA instead of native elements | Worse than none | Use native HTML first |\r\n| `<button>` with no `type` inside a form | Submits the form accidentally | `type=\"button\"` |\r\n| Using `javascript:` in `href` | Insecure and inaccessible | Use a real URL and an event listener |\r\n| Relying on `required` and `pattern` alone | Easy to bypass | Validate on the server |\r\n| Inline `style` and `on*` handlers everywhere | Hard to maintain and conflicts with CSP | Classes and event listeners |\r\n| Obsolete presentation attributes | Not supported consistently | Use CSS |\r\n| Case or path typos in `src` and `href` | 404 errors | Check paths and the Network tab |\r\n\r\n**Part 3 summary.** You can choose between attributes, CSS, and JavaScript, tell attributes from properties, read and change attributes in JavaScript, use attribute selectors, apply accessibility and performance practices, avoid common security problems, and debug attribute mistakes.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Part 4 of 4: Check Your Understanding and Practice\r\n\r\n### Section A: Questions and answers\r\n\r\n**Q1. What is an attribute, and where does it go in an element?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** An attribute gives an element extra information or settings, written as `name=\"value\"`. It goes only in the opening tag (never the closing tag), separated from other attributes by spaces. For example, in `<a href=\"about.html\" class=\"button\">`, `href` and `class` are attributes.\r\n\r\n**Q2. Why is `<input type=\"checkbox\" checked=\"false\">` still ticked, and how do you untick it?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** `checked` is a boolean attribute, so it is on whenever it is present, whatever its value. To untick the box, remove the attribute entirely (or, in JavaScript, set the property `input.checked = false`).\r\n\r\n**Q3. What is the difference between `id` and `class`?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** An `id` must be unique on the page and identifies a single element, which is useful for link targets, `<label for>`, ARIA references, and scripts. A `class` can be shared by many elements, and one element can have several (separated by spaces), which is how groups of elements are styled and selected.\r\n\r\n**Q4. What is the difference between `disabled` and `readonly` on a form control?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** A `disabled` control cannot be focused or changed, and its value is **not submitted** with the form. A `readonly` control cannot be edited but can be focused, and its value **is submitted**. Neither is a security control, since a user can edit both in the browser.\r\n\r\n**Q5. A form field's value never reaches the server. What are two likely causes?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** The control has no `name` attribute (controls without a name are not submitted), or it is `disabled`. Other causes include an unchecked checkbox or radio (not submitted), or the control sitting outside the form without a `form` attribute linking it.\r\n\r\n**Q6. Why must every informative image have `alt` text, and what do you use for a decorative image?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** `alt` gives screen reader users and anyone who cannot see the image a text equivalent, and it appears if the image fails to load. It should describe the image's purpose. For a decorative image use an empty alt (`alt=\"\"`), so assistive technology skips it. Omitting `alt` altogether is an error, because a screen reader may read the file name instead.\r\n\r\n**Q7. What do `width` and `height` on an `<img>` do, and what does `loading=\"lazy\"` do?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** `width` and `height` give the image's intrinsic size so the browser reserves space before it loads, which stops the page from jumping (layout shift). `loading=\"lazy\"` delays loading until the image is near the viewport, which saves data and speeds up the first view. Do not lazy-load the main image at the top of the page.\r\n\r\n**Q8. When should you use `target=\"_blank\"`, and what should you add to such links?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Use it sparingly, when opening a new tab genuinely helps (for example, leaving a form half-completed to read a policy). Add `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` to stop the new page gaining access to the opener (reverse tabnabbing) and to limit referrer leakage, even though modern browsers apply `noopener` by default. Also tell users when a link opens a new tab.\r\n\r\n**Q9. Why is `onclick=\"...\"` in HTML discouraged, and what is the better alternative?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** Inline event handlers mix behaviour into markup, are harder to maintain, and conflict with a strict Content Security Policy (which blocks inline scripts), reducing protection against cross-site scripting. The better approach is to select the element and attach behaviour with `addEventListener` in an external script.\r\n\r\n**Q10. A user's name is inserted into a `title=\"...\"` attribute without escaping. What can go wrong, and how do you prevent it?**\r\n\r\n**Answer:** If the name contains a double quote, it can end the attribute early and let an attacker add new attributes such as an event handler (cross-site scripting). Prevent it by escaping output for the attribute context on the server, or in JavaScript by creating elements and setting properties (`a.title = value`) rather than building HTML strings, and by adding a Content Security Policy as an extra layer.\r\n\r\n### Section B: Practical (one task, step by step)\r\n\r\n**Goal:** build a Harbor Goods \"order and contact\" page that uses the most important attributes on links, images, forms, ARIA, and custom data, then test, inspect, and validate it.\r\n\r\n**What you need:** a computer, a web browser, and a plain text editor (Notepad on Windows, TextEdit in plain text mode on Mac, or a code editor such as Visual Studio Code). Allow about 60 minutes.\r\n\r\n**Step 1: Create the project folder and file.**\r\n\r\n1. Open your **Documents** (or **Desktop**) folder.\r\n2. Create a folder named `harbor-attributes`.\r\n3. Inside it, create a folder named `images`.\r\n4. In your editor, create a new file and **Save As** `index.html` inside `harbor-attributes`. Make sure it saves as UTF-8 and ends in `.html` (on Windows, turn on **File name extensions** in File Explorer's **View** menu if you cannot see the ending).\r\n\r\n**Step 2: Add the page skeleton.**\r\n\r\nType this into `index.html` and save with `Ctrl + S` (or `Cmd + S`):\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html lang=\"en\">\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\r\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\r\n    <title>Order and Contact | Harbor Goods</title>\r\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"Order the Coastal Pro life jacket or contact Harbor Goods.\">\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nOpen the file in your browser by double-clicking it. Keep the browser open. After each step below, save in the editor, switch to the browser, and press `F5` to refresh.\r\n\r\n**Step 3: Add a header with links, and use `id`, `class`, and `aria-current`.**\r\n\r\nGo **between `<body>` and `</body>`** and add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n    <header>\r\n      <h1 id=\"top\">Harbor Goods</h1>\r\n      <nav aria-label=\"Main\">\r\n        <ul>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#product\" class=\"nav-link\" aria-current=\"page\">Product</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#order\" class=\"nav-link\">Order</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#contact\" class=\"nav-link\">Contact</a></li>\r\n          <li>\r\n            <a href=\"https://example.com\" class=\"nav-link\" target=\"_blank\"\r\n               rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Safety guide (new tab)</a>\r\n          </li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n      </nav>\r\n    </header>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. You should see a heading and four links. The first three will not work yet, because their targets do not exist. Click the last link. It should open a new tab.\r\n\r\n**Step 4: Add the product section with an image and its attributes.**\r\n\r\nDirectly **after `</header>`**, add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n    <main>\r\n      <section id=\"product\">\r\n        <h2>Coastal Pro Life Jacket</h2>\r\n        <p>\r\n          A lightweight, tested jacket for sailing, kayaking, and fishing.\r\n          Hover over <abbr title=\"Personal flotation device\">PFD</abbr> to see its meaning.\r\n        </p>\r\n\r\n        <img src=\"images/life-jacket.jpg\"\r\n             alt=\"Red adult life jacket with a front zip and reflective strips\"\r\n             width=\"400\" height=\"300\"\r\n             loading=\"lazy\">\r\n      </section>\r\n    </main>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The image will appear broken, showing its alt text. That is expected, because the file does not exist yet.\r\n\r\n**Step 5: Fix the image and test your attributes.**\r\n\r\n1. Find any small JPEG photo on your computer.\r\n2. Copy it into the `images` folder and rename it exactly `life-jacket.jpg`.\r\n3. Refresh. The picture should appear.\r\n4. **Experiment A:** in the editor, change `alt=\"Red adult...\"` to `alt=\"\"`, save, and refresh. Nothing visible changes, but a screen reader would now skip the image. Restore the original alt text.\r\n5. **Experiment B:** change the `src` to `images/Life-Jacket.jpg` (capital letters), save, and refresh. The image may break on case-sensitive systems. Change it back, and note why lowercase names are safest.\r\n6. Hover over **PFD**. You should see the `title` text as a tooltip.\r\n\r\n**Step 6: Add a product list using custom `data-` attributes.**\r\n\r\nInside `<section id=\"product\">`, **after the image**, add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n        <h3>Available sizes</h3>\r\n        <ul id=\"sizes\">\r\n          <li class=\"size\" data-size=\"s\" data-price=\"45\" data-in-stock=\"true\">Small</li>\r\n          <li class=\"size\" data-size=\"m\" data-price=\"48\" data-in-stock=\"true\">Medium</li>\r\n          <li class=\"size\" data-size=\"l\" data-price=\"50\" data-in-stock=\"false\">Large</li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. You will see three list items. The data attributes are invisible, but they are stored on the elements. You will use them in Step 12.\r\n\r\n**Step 7: Add the order form with labels and validation attributes.**\r\n\r\nDirectly **after `</section>`** (still inside `<main>`), add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n      <section id=\"order\">\r\n        <h2>Order your jacket</h2>\r\n        <form action=\"#\" method=\"post\">\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"fullname\">Full name</label><br>\r\n            <input type=\"text\" id=\"fullname\" name=\"fullname\"\r\n                   required minlength=\"2\" maxlength=\"60\" autocomplete=\"name\">\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"email\">Email address</label><br>\r\n            <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\"\r\n                   required autocomplete=\"email\" inputmode=\"email\"\r\n                   placeholder=\"you@example.com\" aria-describedby=\"email-help\">\r\n            <small id=\"email-help\">We only use this to send your receipt.</small>\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"size\">Size</label><br>\r\n            <select id=\"size\" name=\"size\" required>\r\n              <option value=\"\">Choose a size</option>\r\n              <option value=\"s\">Small</option>\r\n              <option value=\"m\">Medium</option>\r\n            </select>\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"qty\">Quantity (1 to 10)</label><br>\r\n            <input type=\"number\" id=\"qty\" name=\"qty\"\r\n                   min=\"1\" max=\"10\" step=\"1\" value=\"1\" inputmode=\"numeric\">\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"code\">Order code (3 letters then 4 digits)</label><br>\r\n            <input type=\"text\" id=\"code\" name=\"code\"\r\n                   pattern=\"[A-Za-z]{3}[0-9]{4}\" maxlength=\"7\"\r\n                   title=\"Three letters followed by four digits, such as ABC1234\">\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <fieldset>\r\n            <legend>Delivery</legend>\r\n            <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"delivery\" value=\"standard\" checked> Standard</label>\r\n            <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"delivery\" value=\"express\"> Express</label>\r\n          </fieldset>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label for=\"notes\">Notes</label><br>\r\n            <textarea id=\"notes\" name=\"notes\" rows=\"3\" cols=\"40\" maxlength=\"300\"></textarea>\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <p>\r\n            <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\" value=\"yes\"> Send me offers</label>\r\n          </p>\r\n\r\n          <button type=\"submit\">Place order</button>\r\n          <button type=\"reset\">Clear</button>\r\n        </form>\r\n      </section>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. Now test each attribute. Write what happens in a notepad:\r\n\r\n1. Click **Place order** with everything empty. Which field does the browser point to, and why? (Answer: a `required` one.)\r\n2. Type `hello` in the email box and submit. What message do you get? (The `type=\"email\"` check.)\r\n3. Type `A` in Full name and submit. What happens? (`minlength`.)\r\n4. Type `12` in Quantity and submit. What happens? (`max`.)\r\n5. Type `AB1` in Order code and submit. What happens? (`pattern`, and the `title` text appears in the message.)\r\n6. Click the words **Full name**. Where does the cursor go? (The `for` and `id` link.)\r\n7. Click **Express** and then **Standard**. Why can you select only one? (The same `name`.)\r\n8. Click **Clear**. What happens? (`type=\"reset\"`.)\r\n\r\n**Step 8: Test `name`, `disabled`, and `readonly`.**\r\n\r\nYou can see what a form would send by using the `get` method, which puts the values in the address bar.\r\n\r\n1. In the editor, change `method=\"post\"` on the form to `method=\"get\"`. Save and refresh.\r\n2. Fill in the form correctly and submit. Look at the address bar. You should see `?fullname=...&email=...&size=...` and so on.\r\n3. Find your `qty` input and add the word `disabled` inside its tag: `<input type=\"number\" id=\"qty\" name=\"qty\" ... disabled>`. Save, refresh, and submit again. Is `qty` in the address bar? (It should not be.)\r\n4. Replace `disabled` with `readonly`. Save, refresh, and submit. Is `qty` in the address bar now? (It should be.)\r\n5. Remove `name=\"notes\"` from the textarea only, save, refresh, type a note, and submit. Is `notes` in the address bar? (It should not be.)\r\n6. **Restore everything:** remove `readonly`, put `name=\"notes\"` back, and change `method` back to `post`.\r\n\r\nThis shows that a control with no `name`, or with `disabled`, is not submitted, and that `readonly` is.\r\n\r\n**Step 9: Add a contact section with email and phone links.**\r\n\r\nAfter the closing `</section>` of the order section (still inside `<main>`), add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n      <section id=\"contact\">\r\n        <h2>Contact us</h2>\r\n        <ul>\r\n          <li><a href=\"mailto:hello@harborgoods.example\">Email: hello@harborgoods.example</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"tel:+15551234567\">Phone: +1 555 123 4567</a></li>\r\n          <li><a href=\"#top\">Back to top</a></li>\r\n        </ul>\r\n      </section>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. Click **Back to top**, then the menu links **Product**, **Order**, and **Contact**. All three should now jump to the right sections, because each `href=\"#...\"` matches an `id`.\r\n\r\n**Step 10: Add a footer.**\r\n\r\nAfter `</main>`, add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n    <footer>\r\n      <p>&copy; 2026 Harbor Goods</p>\r\n    </footer>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh.\r\n\r\n**Step 11: Add a menu button using ARIA and `hidden`.**\r\n\r\n1. Inside `<header>`, **before the `<nav>`**, add:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n      <button type=\"button\" id=\"help-btn\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"help\">Help</button>\r\n      <div id=\"help\" hidden>\r\n        <p>Need help? Use the contact section below or email us.</p>\r\n      </div>\r\n```\r\n\r\n2. Just **before the closing `</body>` tag**, add this script:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n    <script>\r\n      const helpBtn = document.querySelector(\"#help-btn\");\r\n      const help = document.querySelector(\"#help\");\r\n\r\n      helpBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n        const open = helpBtn.getAttribute(\"aria-expanded\") === \"true\";\r\n        helpBtn.setAttribute(\"aria-expanded\", String(!open));\r\n        help.hidden = open;\r\n      });\r\n    </script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n3. Save and refresh. The help text should be hidden. Click **Help**: it should appear, and click again to hide it.\r\n4. Press `F12`, open the **Elements** panel, and click the Help button again while watching the `button` and `div`. You should see `aria-expanded` change between `false` and `true`, and the `hidden` attribute appear and disappear. This is attributes being changed by JavaScript.\r\n\r\n**Step 12: Read the `data-` attributes with JavaScript.**\r\n\r\nInside the same `<script>`, **after the help code**, add:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n      document.querySelectorAll(\".size\").forEach((item) => {\r\n        const price = item.dataset.price;\r\n        const inStock = item.dataset.inStock === \"true\";\r\n        item.textContent += \" - $\" + price + (inStock ? \"\" : \" (out of stock)\");\r\n      });\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. Each size should now show its price, and Large should say \"out of stock\". Note that `data-in-stock` became `dataset.inStock`, and its value is a string, which is why we compare it with `\"true\"`.\r\n\r\n**Step 13: Style by attribute.**\r\n\r\nAdd a `<style>` block inside the `<head>`, after the description line:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n    <style>\r\n      a[target=\"_blank\"]::after { content: \" \\2197\"; }\r\n      input:required { border: 2px solid #0b3d5c; }\r\n      input:invalid  { border-color: #b00020; }\r\n      [data-in-stock=\"false\"] { color: #777; text-decoration: line-through; }\r\n      [hidden] { display: none; }\r\n    </style>\r\n```\r\n\r\nSave and refresh. The new-tab link should have an arrow, required inputs should have a blue border (red if invalid), and the Large size should be struck through. These rules select elements **by their attributes**, with no extra class needed.\r\n\r\n**Step 14: Inspect the attributes.**\r\n\r\n1. Press `F12` and open the **Elements** panel.\r\n2. Click the **Select element** arrow at the top-left of the panel, then click the email input on the page. In the panel, note every attribute it has.\r\n3. Double-click the `type=\"email\"` text in the Elements panel and change it to `type=\"text\"`. Click into the field on the page and note the change in how the field behaves. (On a phone, the keyboard would change too.) Refresh to undo.\r\n4. Right-click the page and choose **View page source**. Find the `data-` attributes. Note that anyone can read them, so they must never hold secrets or prices you trust on the server.\r\n\r\n**Step 15: Break things on purpose, then validate.**\r\n\r\nMake each of these changes one at a time, then undo it:\r\n\r\n1. Remove the `for` from one label. Click the label text. Does the field still focus?\r\n2. Change one input's `id` to match another input's `id` (a duplicate), then run the validator and read the error.\r\n3. Remove the quotes from `class=\"nav-link\"` on one link and write `class=nav-link extra`. In the Elements panel, see what the browser made of it.\r\n4. Write `checked=\"false\"` on the checkbox and refresh.\r\n\r\nThen validate the final page: go to `validator.w3.org`, choose **Validate by Direct Input**, paste the whole file, and click **Check**. Read messages from the top down. Fix any errors and check again until there are none.\r\n\r\n**Step 16: Security review.**\r\n\r\nRead your own source and answer:\r\n\r\n1. Which attributes did you use that a user can edit in their browser (for example `required`, `pattern`, `max`, `readonly`, `data-price`)? What would your server still have to check?\r\n2. Is there any `javascript:` URL or inline `onclick` in your page? (There should be none.)\r\n3. Does every `target=\"_blank\"` link have `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`?\r\n4. Are there any secrets in attributes or comments? (There should be none.)\r\n5. Where would you need `integrity` and `crossorigin` if you added a script from a CDN?\r\n\r\n**Step 17: Reflect.**\r\n\r\nAnswer in a notepad (one or two lines each):\r\n\r\n1. Which attributes were boolean, and how did you turn them off?\r\n2. Which attributes connect two elements together, and how?\r\n3. Which attributes affect what the server receives?\r\n4. Which attributes help accessibility?\r\n5. Which step surprised you most, and why?\r\n\r\n**Finished check.** Your page should have: a valid skeleton; navigation links that jump to sections (`href` and `id` matching), plus one new-tab link with `rel`; an image with `alt`, `width`, `height`, and `loading`; a labelled form that uses `required`, `minlength`, `maxlength`, `min`, `max`, `pattern`, `type`, `autocomplete`, `inputmode`, and `aria-describedby`; custom `data-` attributes read by JavaScript; a help button that toggles `aria-expanded` and `hidden`; CSS attribute selectors; and no errors in the validator.\r\n\r\n### Self-check\r\n\r\n| Skill | Done |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Write attributes correctly, with quotes, in the opening tag | ☐ |\r\n| Tell boolean, enumerated, global, ARIA, event, and data attributes apart | ☐ |\r\n| Avoid the `checked=\"false\"` trap | ☐ |\r\n| Use `id` and `class` correctly | ☐ |\r\n| Write link attributes, including `target` and `rel` | ☐ |\r\n| Write image attributes, including `alt`, size, `srcset`, and `loading` | ☐ |\r\n| Build forms with `name`, `value`, labels, and the right `type` | ☐ |\r\n| Use validation attributes and remember to validate on the server | ☐ |\r\n| Explain `disabled` versus `readonly` | ☐ |\r\n| Add `data-*` attributes and read them with `dataset` | ☐ |\r\n| Use ARIA attributes and the first rule of ARIA | ☐ |\r\n| Use `tabindex` correctly | ☐ |\r\n| Distinguish attributes from properties | ☐ |\r\n| Read and change attributes with JavaScript | ☐ |\r\n| Use CSS attribute selectors | ☐ |\r\n| Avoid obsolete and inline attributes | ☐ |\r\n| Avoid attribute-related security mistakes (XSS, `javascript:`, tabnabbing) | ☐ |\r\n| Debug attributes with the Elements panel and the validator | ☐ |\r\n\r\n**Lesson complete.**";
